      "text": "[asy]\nunitsize(8mm);\nreal f(real x) {\n  return(sqrt((x - 5)^2 + 16) - x + 5);\n}\ndraw(xradius(5)*Circle((0,0),5),linewidth(0.7));\ndraw(ellipse((5,0),5,4),linewidth(0.7));\nlabel(\"$A$\",(5,-1),W);\nlabel(\"$O$\",(5,0),S);\nlabel(\"$F$\",(0,4),N);\nlabel(\"$2p$\",(6,0),S);\nlabel(\"$2a$\",(10,0),S);\nlabel(\"$2a,e$\",(14,0),S);\n[/asy]\n\nThe major axis is 10, so $2a = 10,$ which means $a=5.$\n\nThe minor axis is 8, so $2b = 8,$ which means $b=4.$\n\nSince $.25 \\cdot b=.25 \\cdot 4=.1$  is less than E, which is $\\frac{8}{5} = 1.6,$\n\nthe circle lies entirely inside the ellipse . To find the radius of the circle that is tangent to the ellipse and centered at one of its foci, we start by determining the characteristics of the ellipse and the properties of the circle.\n\nGiven:\n- The major axis length is 10, so \\(2a = 10\\) and thus \\(a = 5\\).\n- The minor axis length is 8, so \\(2b = 8\\) and thus \\(b = 4\\).\n\nThe ellipse's semi-major axis \\(a = \\frac{8}{5}\\) and the distance from the center to each focus \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{25} - 16} = \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{25} - \\frac{400}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{64 - 400}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{64 - 400}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{64 - 400}{25}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{64 - 400}{25}} = \\frac{7}{5}\\).\n\nThis means \\(e = \\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{\\frac{7}{5}}{\\frac{8}{5}} = \\frac{7}{8}\\). But here, the problem states that \\(e \\approx .8\\), so it seems there might be a need to verify the exact values for \\(e\\). However, for simplicity, we will proceed with the given approximate value of \\(e = \\frac{7}{8}\\).\n\nThe circle is tangent to the ellipse, which means the distance from the focus to the center of the circle (which is also the center of the ellipse in this case) plus the radius equals the distance from the center of the ellipse to the point of tangency on the ellipse.\n\nThe distance from the center of the ellipse to the focus is \\(c = \\frac{7}{5}\\). Therefore, the radius \\(r\\) of the circle is the distance from the focus to the center of the ellipse minus the distance of the point of tangency from the center of the ellipse.\n\nFrom the problem and the given configuration, we can infer that the radius is half the difference between the semi-major axis \\(a\\) and the distance from the center to the focus. This is a well-known geometric property of ellipses. So, the radius \\(r\\) is:\n\\[ r = a - c = 5 - \\frac{7}{5} = \\frac{25}{5} - \\frac{7}{5} = \\frac{18}{5} = 3.6. \\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
