      "text": "Let $r$ be the radius and let $F$ be the focus.\n$F$ is also the center of the semicircle.\nLet $D$ be the point is the ellipse intersects.\nThe diagram of the setup:\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.4cm);\n\ndraw(Circle((0,0),1));\ndraw(Arc((0,0),(1,0),(0,0.80));\ndraw((0,0)--(8,0));\ndraw(Fred((0,0.80)),EndArrow);\ndraw(Circle((0,-0.5),0.5));\n\nlabel(\"$D$\",(0.80,0.80),NE);\nlabel(\"$F$\",(0,0.80),N);\nlabel(\"$A$\",(8,0),E);\nlabel(\"$B$\",(0,0),S);\nlabel(\"$R$\",(1,0.80),N);\nlabel(\"$E$\",(1.75,0.57),NE);\n[/asy]\nWe see that $AD$ is tangent to the semicircle.\nBy the tangent leg theorem of circles, $FD$ is also tangent to the semicircle.\nLet $n$ be the radius of the semicircle.\n$\\angle DFE$ is an isosceles triangle (since $DF=FD=FD$), so by the congruence theorem, $DE=FN=FE=n$.\nNow let's look at right triangle $BDE$.\nHere is the diagram of the same setup, so that the setup is identical:\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.4cm);\n\ndraw(Circle((0,0),1));\ndraw(Arc((0,0),(1,0),(0,0.80));\ndraw((0,0)--(8,0));\ndraw(Fred((0,0.80)),EndArrow);\ndraw(Circle((0,-0.5),0.5));\n\nlabel(\"$D$\",(0.80,0.80),NE);\nlabel(\"$F$\",(0,0.80),N);\nlabel(\"$A$\",(1.80,0),E);\nlabel(\"$B$\",(0,0),S);\nlabel(\"$R$\",(1,0.80),N);\nlabel(\"$E$\",(1.75,0.57),NE);\n[/asy]\nFrom Pythagorean theorem, $BD^2=AB^2+AD^2=8^2+(2n)^2$.\nSince $\\angle EFM=90^\\circ$, then by the tangent-secant theorem:\n$\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{2n}{2n^2+8}$.\nFinally, we get that $n=\\boxed{\\frac{8}{5}}$. The answer is \\frac{8}{5}",
