    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "$ c^{2}+5^{2}=10^{2}$ $ c=3$ radius of circle $ = \\sqrt{r^2+9} = \\boxed{\\sqrt{10}}$.\n\nTo begin, determining the maximum horizontal and vertical radii is the first step. The width of the ellipse is 10, and the height of the ellipse is 8. Now that we know the two radii, we can write the ellipse equation as $(x^2)/10^2 + (y^2)/8^2 = 1$ Using the Foci definition for an ellipse, we will place major and minor axis lengths as if they were equal in length. $(a^2)-c^2= b^2$ By knowing the major and minor axis lengths we can find a and c a quadratic equation. We want to solve for $c^2$ so we divide both sides by , $a^2$ Then after expanding and rearranging, $c^2= (a^2)-b^2$ Therefore we have $c^2= 100-8^2=64$ $c= \\sqrt{64}$ To find the radius of the circle which is tangent to the ellipse we can use the Foci definition for a circle; a circle centered at a focus of the ellipse with radius $c$ is tangent and does not overlap the ellipse. $c=\\sqrt{r^2+9}$ $r=\\sqrt{c^2-9}$ $r=\\sqrt{64-9}$ $r=\\boxed{\\sqrt{10}}$\n\nyep...I got the exact same answer as @2 above, and the answer key shows the same answer as us. they have a great coordinator in sir d\n\nThis problem looks like it can be solved using circles. Draw an ellipse with a standard deviation of 1 = $y=\\pm \\sqrt{10- x^{2}/8}$ Draw a circle tangent to two points on the ellipse, let it have radius of c. $c^{2}+5^{2}=10^{2}$ The requirements for a circle in relation to the ellipse are $c=\\sqrt{r^{2}+9}$ $r=\\sqrt{c^{2}-9}$ $r=\\sqrt{64-9}=\\sqrt{56}$ This is interpreted through spherical coordinates. (~the complex number line makes a sphere)\n\nTo better prove upper and lower base balls, one can kinda carefully extend your $c$ to $(c+1012/4)^{2}=100^{2}$ notice the asymptotic lower base balls. (encircle the exclamation point).",
